---
// Deep cleaning page, rebuilt from the SECOND deep-cleaning brief (2026-09-08).
// Section choices and every "don't invent this" call trace back to
// content-plans/deep-cleaning.md.
//
// WHAT THE NEW BRIEF RESOLVED, against the three 🚩 blockers the first build
// shipped with:
//  1. ✅ Meta description. The old brief pasted END OF LEASE copy onto this
//     page and the build ran a written-from-the-H1 placeholder instead. The
//     new brief supplies real deep-cleaning copy — it ships verbatim.
//  2. ✅ "The task list is published on this page." It now is: §5 below is
//     the full room-by-room inclusions list, five rooms plus the two
//     exclusion groups. That FAQ answer no longer over-promises.
//  3. ✅ The "[X] days" lead-time FAQ is gone from the brief entirely, so
//     there is nothing held back for an unfilled number any more.
//  4. ✅ Credibility signals are back. That section was REMOVED at TLB's
//     direction last time, because the old brief's row for it was empty and
//     the first build had filled it on the sibling page's premise. The new
//     brief specifies it outright ("before and after images from genuine TLB
//     deep cleans, with client permission and no identifying detail"), so it
//     returns — as reserved slots, since no such images exist yet.
//  5. ✅ "Where we clean" is specified for the first time, with its own
//     three-region town list, so the "don't pick a suburb list silently"
//     hold from the first build is lifted. See §12 — it lifts it by making
//     the site's list disagreement bigger, not smaller.
//
// STILL NEEDS TEAGAN'S SIGN-OFF (the brief's own [CONFIRM] markers). These
// ship as copy rather than being held back, because the brief wrote them as
// finished sentences for review on the built page — unlike the old "[X]
// days", which was a literal blank nobody had filled. Each is flagged at its
// own definition below:
//   - §5 / §5b's full inclusions AND exclusions lists.
//   - FAQ "How long does a deep clean take?" — the duration estimate.
//   - FAQ "What isn't included in a deep clean?" — the exclusions list again.
//   - FAQ "Do you bring your own products and equipment?" — product range.
//
// Blocked-on-media sections (§8 before/after gallery, §9 video, §11
// testimonials) are built as RESERVED SLOTS — labelled Placeholder frames
// and bracketed [TBC] briefing text, obviously unfinished on sight, zero
// layout shift when the real assets land. Never fill one with invented
// photos, quotes or names.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/sections/SiteHeader.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/sections/Hero.astro';
import TrustBar from '../../components/sections/TrustBar.astro';
import Backdrop from '../../components/ui/Backdrop.astro';
import TextBlock from '../../components/sections/TextBlock.astro';
import PathwayCards from '../../components/sections/PathwayCards.astro';
import ContentGrid from '../../components/sections/ContentGrid.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/sections/Callout.astro';
import ComparisonTable from '../../components/sections/ComparisonTable.astro';
// Before/after. Both pairs are REAL TLB job photography.
import BeforeAfter from '../../components/sections/BeforeAfter.astro';
// Before/after pairs — real TLB job photos. The `sample-` files are flat
// illustrations and stay only where no real pair exists for that subject yet.
import VideoFeature from '../../components/sections/VideoFeature.astro';
import GoogleReviewSlider from '../../components/sections/GoogleReviewSlider.astro';
import { googleBusinessProfileUrl, googleReviews } from '../../data/googleReviews';
import TagCloud from '../../components/sections/TagCloud.astro';
import { locationGroups } from '../../data/locations';
import Faq from '../../components/sections/Faq.astro';
import CallToAction from '../../components/sections/CallToAction.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/sections/SiteFooter.astro';
import logoImg from '../../assets/logo/tlb-logo-horizontal.png';
// §3 situation cards — one photo per card, named for the situation it shows.
// §2 backdrop, behind the Cream scrim (the homepage's — this page's folder has no wide backdrop of its own).
import footerLogoImg from '../../assets/logo/tlb-logo-square.png';
// Site chrome — shared with index.astro and house-cleaning.astro.
import {
  primaryNav,
  headerNav,
  headerSecondaryCta,
  footerServiceLinks,
  footerContact,
  footerCopyright,
  quoteCta,
} from '../../data/navigation';
import { trustBarCards } from '../../data/trust';
import { howItWorks } from '../../data/brand-lines';
import { localBusiness } from '../../data/business';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';
import { getPage } from '../../lib/cms';
import * as section from '../../lib/cms-schemas';
import { sized, highlightColumn } from '../../lib/cms-schemas';

// §0 Page metadata. Title and description both drafted in the brief and
// shipping verbatim — the description that used to be a placeholder here is
// now real copy (see the file header).
//
// In `pageMeta`:
// ⚠️ REVERTED TO THE KEYWORD WORKBOOK 16 Sep 2026, on TLB's instruction to
// ship the workbook copy verbatim. 145 characters, matching the workbook's
// own length column exactly. This undoes the 14 Sep copy-review rewrite.
//
// What the revert costs, recorded so it is not lost:
//  1. The mapped primary `house deep cleaning services` is out of the
//     description again. The 14 Sep rewrite existed to put it in for the
//     first time; the workbook line contains it nowhere. The title still
//     carries it verbatim.
//  2. No service area at all. The standing geo phrase "across the Northern
//     Rivers and the Tweed" is gone, so this page's meta names no
//     footprint while the hero, the body and the town list all do. Note
//     this also retires the open question about whether the geo phrase
//     should stop at the Tweed on the Coolangatta, Palm Beach and Burleigh
//     Heads rows: there is no geo phrase here to extend.
//  3. "Book today" is a harder CTA than this page supports. BFD §13.2
//     names deep cleans as work instant pricing works badly for, which is
//     why the CTA below was overridden away from "instant". The meta now
//     says book, the page says get a fast quote.
// In `pageMeta`:
// Domain still a placeholder, same as the other pages — confirm before launch.

// ⚠️ PAGE-LOCAL CTA LABEL. The shared `quoteCta` still reads "Get an instant
// quote" everywhere, and on THIS page that contradicted the sentence next to
// it: the hero lead, the §10 heading and the booking copy all say quoting is
// fast, not instant, because BFD §13.2 names deep cleans as work instant
// pricing works badly for. The button was the last thing still saying
// "instant".
//
// Overridden here rather than in navigation.ts because that export serves
// every page, including the genuinely instant-priceable ones. The site-wide
// split is still the Phase 2.3 / step 7 job in WEBSITE-UPDATE-PLAN-BFD-V2.md
// — this is the deep-clean page taking its own half of it early. `href` is
// unchanged, so it routes exactly where it did.
//
// Header and footer chrome keep the shared label deliberately: that is site
// navigation, not this page's argument.
const pageQuoteCta = { ...quoteCta, label: 'Get a fast quote' };

// TRUST BAR: the shared `trustBarCards` from data/trust.ts, same as the other
// fifty pages. Rendered directly below — this page holds no local set.
//
// ── REMOVED by request (September 2026) ───────────────────────────────────
//
// This page used to be the ONLY one of fifty-one running a page-local trust
// bar. It replaced the four shared cards with four process promises ("Set day,
// set team", "Told first" and so on) to avoid the two unsourced figures the
// shared set carries: "98% of our clients stay with us" and "Over 100 local
// homes, hosts and businesses".
//
// The reasoning was sound and is worth keeping on the record, because the
// underlying issue has NOT gone away. BFD §2.4 marks three of the four shared
// cards "Confirm", and those two numbers are exactly what a competitor or the
// ACCC can ask TLB to substantiate. What the override got wrong was the
// SCOPE: one page quietly telling a different story about the same business
// is a worse problem than the one it solved, and it is the same divergence
// data/trust.ts was extracted to end (see its own header).
//
// ⚠️ SO THE OPEN QUESTION MOVED, IT DID NOT CLOSE. If those two figures
// cannot be verified, the fix belongs in data/trust.ts where it reaches all
// fifty-one pages at once — not here. The process-promise wording is in git
// history if it is wanted as the site-wide replacement.

// §1 Hero — drafted in full, `split-single-image` on the page's one hero
// photo. Image right via `imagePosition`, `ratio` matching the asset's
// native 1920x1080 so the cut-out figure isn't cropped once the layout goes
// single-column. The figure faces left, into the copy, which is the way
// round it wants to be with the image on the right.
//
// The old brief's 31-word overview (flagged in the first build as two
// sentences doing one sentence's job) has been rewritten by the new one —
// the second sentence is now the page's three-part promise, which §10 then
// expands under those same words. No longer a flag, just better copy.
//
// CTA sits here, not on §2's TextBlock: Hero.astro's `cta` prop is required.
// Same ruling as house-cleaning.astro.
// ⚠️ H1 rewritten again, 16 Sep 2026, to carry the mapped primary
// `deep cleaning` in its exact form and in first position — "One deep clean
// to reset the whole house" only ever had the `deep clean` variant. The
// one-off framing it drops is still carried by the page title and by the
// lead's first sentence, so nothing is lost. The lead names the service and
// the region and picks up `move in cleaning`, with the emotional hook and
// The Line both intact behind it.
//
// In `hero`, on "A TLB cleaner crouched in a walk-in shower, wiping down the tiles and glass":
// A real TLB photo since 6 Oct 2026 (the same frame as the tile and grout
// hero), cropped ~1.15:1 from the bottom of a tall video frame to match
// the 560px desktop panel. `ratio` sets the panel height (the image box
// takes that aspect at the panel's width); '4/3' keeps the 560px floor
// in charge on desktop and gives mobile a frame that keeps her head in.

// §2 Definition paragraph — unchanged between the two briefs. The
// answer-target block for `house deep cleaning services`; stays the first
// prose after the hero.
// The middle sentence was added 14 Sep 2026. It absorbs three variants the
// page had no coverage for at all — `once off house cleaning`, `one off
// cleaning` and `move in cleaning` — and it does it the way a person
// clarifying would, rather than as a list of search terms.

// §3 Situation cards — copy verbatim, split into title (the opening
// statement) + description (the rest). Cards 1 and 2 unchanged from the
// first brief; card 3's line is now "Fair enough" rather than "Fair", and
// the brief itself now specifies the destination this build had to infer
// last time ("anchor link to the comparison table below").
//
// Section heading/accent and the three CTA labels are NOT in the brief —
// placeholder structural copy. The three photos each show the situation
// their card describes.
//
// In `pathwayCards`, on "Cleaner tidying the bottles in a tiled shower niche":
// Anchor target is #compare, set on the wrapper in the template below
// — ComparisonTable takes no id prop, and adding one just for an
// anchor isn't worth a shared-component change. Label follows the
// photo, not the brief line: the shot is a homeowner weighing it up at
// her kitchen bench, not a side-by-side chart (which no photograph can
// show). Doubles as alt text.

// §5 What's included in a deep clean — THE new section, and the one that
// makes the FAQ's "the task list is published on this page" true.
//
// ⚠️ [CONFIRM the full inclusions and exclusions list with Teagan before
// publishing.] The brief's own marker. This is the page's central promise and
// the thing a client will hold TLB to on the day, task by task — it needs a
// read-through by whoever actually scopes these jobs, not just a copy
// approval.
//
// Component choice: ContentGrid `text` cells, not ServiceBlocks `icon-grid`.
// ServiceBlocks would be the obvious shape for five labelled groups, but its
// cells are icon-led and ServiceIcon.astro has no room icons at all (idea,
// spark, bloom, puzzle, target, chart-pie, chart-bars, house, suitcase, key,
// spray-bottle, office) — five rooms would have shared two or three
// unrelated glyphs, which reads worse than no icons. TextBlock with
// `{ list }` entries was the other candidate, rejected for the opposite
// reason to §6's: one column of five long comma-runs reads as a wall, where
// a 2-up grid lets someone find their own room.
//
// Each room's tasks run as the brief wrote them — one comma-separated
// sentence, not re-broken into bullets. That's a copy decision (the brief
// chose prose over a checklist look) and re-formatting it would change how
// exhaustive the list reads.
//
// Layout: 5 cells in 2 columns leaves an orphan, so "Throughout" — the
// shortest, and the only one that isn't a room — takes `span: 2` and closes
// the grid as a full-width row.

// §5b The two exclusion groups — Callout, not two more grid cells.
//
// They are the opposite of everything above them, and an inset panel that
// reads as an interruption is exactly Callout's stated job. A sixth grid
// cell, by contrast, gets skimmed as a sixth room. `tone="note"` — this is
// "worth knowing", not "don't do this".
//
// ⚠️ Same [CONFIRM] as §5, and the higher-stakes half of it: an exclusion a
// client didn't expect is the complaint that actually happens. The FAQ "What
// isn't included in a deep clean?" restates this list, so the two have to be
// settled together and kept in step.
// The third line and the CTA were added 14 Sep 2026. `deep cleaning
// checklist` is 170/mo with an AI Overview firing, and the guide it points at
// has almost nothing linking to it.
//
// The link runs through Callout's existing `cta` prop rather than inline
// markup: Callout renders each body string inside <p>{paragraph}</p>, so
// Astro escapes any HTML in it. The anchor text still carries the phrase,
// which is the part that matters, and no component changed to get it.

// §6 When people book one — the same six occasions as the first brief, under
// the brief's own heading now ("When people book one"; "The moments this
// comes up" was this build's placeholder).
//
// Six labelled occasions, each a short title plus a one-line gloss: grid
// content, not a paragraph. ContentGrid at 3 columns (2 rows of 3) rather
// than a TextBlock bullet list, which would flatten each title/gloss pair
// into one run-on line.
//
// In `momentsBlocks`, on "Moving in":
// Second sentence added 14 Sep 2026. `move in cleaning` is 290/mo and the
// page had no coverage of it; naming the term here also does the tile's
// emotional work, which a keyword insertion usually cannot.
// In `momentsBlocks`, on "Before it goes to market":
// Extended 14 Sep 2026, to give this tile the same concreteness as the
// others around it.

// §7 Deep clean, regular clean or bond clean — the comparison table, with
// ALL-NEW content. The first build's version came from a design mockup and
// compared task coverage (inside cupboards, skirting, oven detail…). The new
// brief replaces it with four rows that compare the SERVICES rather than the
// tasks: what each one is, the standard it's held to, when you'd book it,
// how long it runs.
//
// That's the right swap now that §5 exists. The old table was doing two jobs
// — "which service do I need" and "what gets cleaned" — and the second has
// moved to §5's actual task list, so this table gets to do only the first.
// It also drops the old "View the full room-by-room checklist" CTA that
// pointed off-page at /guides/what-is-included-in-a-deep-clean/: the
// checklist is on this page now, directly above it.
//
// `cornerLabel` names the question the table answers, since PathwayCards
// card 3 lands people here from "I'm not sure which I need".
//
// LAYOUT NOTE, carried over from the first build: the original mockup put
// heading/lead/CTA in a left-hand panel BESIDE the table. That was built as
// an optional `split` layout on ComparisonTable and reverted after measuring
// it — at a 1200px container a side panel left the 4-column table ~848px
// against the 999px it needs, pushing the third compared service off behind
// a horizontal scroll. Stacked, it fits exactly. Don't re-add the split
// without re-measuring: these rows are longer text than the old ones.
//
// In `comparisonTable`, on "Who decides it passed":
// Replaced the old "How long" row per the updated brief. Duration was the
// weakest of the four distinctions — every answer was a hedge, and the
// FAQ below already gives a real estimate. Who signs off is the row that
// actually changes how someone should book: on the first two columns the
// client decides when it is done, on the third somebody else does.

// §8 Credibility signals — RESERVED SLOTS, no images exist yet.
//
// This section was removed from the first build at TLB's direction, because
// the old brief's row for it was empty and the build had filled it with
// before/after slots on the sibling page's premise. The new brief specifies
// it in almost exactly those terms — "before and after images from genuine
// TLB deep cleans, with client permission and no identifying detail" — so it
// returns. The earlier guess turned out to be right; it just wasn't briefed.
//
// Two before/after pairs, which is the minimum that reads as a pattern
// rather than one lucky room.
//
// These frames are REAL TLB JOB PHOTOGRAPHS, published with the client's
// permission and with nothing identifying in frame — no house numbers, street
// signs, mail, people, or names on doors. That is why this section carries no
// "illustrative samples" note. Do NOT wire the `sample-` files back in without
// restoring a note that says so: a fabricated "before and after from a real
// job" is a false trust claim, not filler.
//
// ⚠️ BOTH PAIRS ARE KITCHEN SURFACES. The shower/bathroom pair this section
// used to carry was dropped rather than shown as an illustration next to a
// photograph, so nothing on this page evidences bathroom work. A real shower
// pair is the outstanding shoot; when it lands it takes slot 2 back.
//
// This is a DRAGGABLE COMPARISON SLIDER (BeforeAfter.astro), not the
// PhotoGallery grid this section used to be — the same change house-cleaning
// made and for the same reason. A grid lays four independent frames out and
// leaves the viewer to hold one in memory while looking at the next; the
// slider makes the PAIR the unit and does the comparison for them, in place.
//
// Shoot each pair from ONE FIXED POSITION. The slider wipes one frame over
// the other in place, so a shifted camera or a different crop reads as a
// glitch rather than as cleaning — a requirement the component cannot
// enforce and the brief has to.
//
// The photo brief. Frames 1 and 2 are DONE. Slot 2 now holds a real cooktop
// pair rather than the bathroom sample, so frames 3 and 4 are still open and
// nothing on this page evidences bathroom work:
//   1. [done] BEFORE — kitchen, genuine TLB deep clean.
//   2. [done] AFTER  — the same kitchen, same angle.
//   3. [TODO] BEFORE — bathroom or shower, genuine TLB deep clean.
//   4. [TODO] AFTER  — the same bathroom, same angle.
//
// ⚠️ Two consent conditions, both from the brief, both on TLB to meet before
// these publish: written client permission, and no identifying detail in
// frame — no house numbers, street signs, mail, people, or names on doors. A
// "before" photo of someone's house is the most sensitive image this site
// would carry.

// §9 Hero video — reserved 16/9 slot. The brief specifies the content ("a
// before-and-after video showing a deep clean"), not the heading;
// `headingLines` is placeholder copy.
//
// ⚠️ Heading rewritten 14 Sep 2026 to carry the keyword and to set an
// accurate expectation of what the frame contains. The frame itself is still
// EMPTY: fill it or remove the section before launch. A promised video that
// isn't there is a trust cost at the exact moment the reader is deciding.

// §10 The Line as a section heading, with the hero's second sentence
// expanded under those same three words.
//
// ⚠️ `fast`, not `instant`. This page read "instant pricing" until v2.0 §13.2
// was applied, and §13.2 names deep cleans specifically as work instant
// pricing "works badly for". The heading, the hero lead and paragraph two of
// bookingBody below all said it, so all three moved together. See
// data/brand-lines.ts for why there are two variants at all.
//
// §1.4 "Managed for you" is prepended at the call site as this block's
// standfirst, the `oneOff` wording because a deep clean has no set day or
// set team to promise.
//
// TextBlock `dark` rather than `light`: it sits between the video slot and
// the testimonials, both of which are light, and it carries the page's
// second CTA. It's also the one block on the page that argues rather than
// lists, so it shouldn't look like §5 or §6. `align="left"` — three
// paragraphs of running prose, not a centred pull quote.
//
// ⚠️ The standfirst is PAGE-LOCAL from 14 Sep 2026, replacing the shared
// `managedForYou.oneOff`. That line was a feature list — date, confirmation,
// message, told first — read in the order TLB does them rather than the order
// a client feels them. The rewrite names the frustration first (BFD §5.2's
// real objection: the organising, not the cleaning) and then resolves it with
// the same four promises. Kept here rather than pushed back into
// brand-lines.ts because every other one-off page still wants the short form.
//
// In `bookingBody`, on its third paragraph ("Cost effective because..."):
// "your home", not "the place", from 14 Sep 2026: it picks up the
// `home deep cleaning services` phrasing, which sits in the same 590/mo
// cluster as the mapped primary but barely appears on a page that is
// otherwise entirely about houses.

// §10b What a deep clean costs — NEW section, added 14 Sep 2026.
//
// Three of the four competitors on page one of `deep cleaning services`
// publish a real number and TLB publishes nothing. This targets roughly
// 120/mo of direct pricing intent (`deep cleaning prices`, `deep clean cost`,
// `how much does a deep clean cost`) plus the second-most-asked People Also
// Ask question on the primary term.
//
// ⚠️ BLOCKING, AND IT IS WHY THIS DOES NOT RENDER YET. The price band is
// Teagan's to supply and nobody else's to estimate. `pricingConfirmed` stays
// false until it lands, which keeps the whole section off the page — a live
// pricing section reading "[Teagan to confirm]" is a worse outcome than no
// pricing section at all, and it is the one place on this page where the
// reserved-slot convention used for the testimonials and the video misfires:
// an unfinished price is read as a price.
//
// The same flag governs FAQ 5 below, so the two move together. While it is
// false the FAQ keeps its current self-contained answer; the moment it is
// true the section appears and the FAQ starts pointing up at it. Flip the
// flag, drop the real band into `pricingBand`, and both land in one change.
// This is the single most valuable outstanding item on the page.
const pricingConfirmed = false;
const pricingBand = '[Teagan to confirm: price band for a 3 bed / 2 bath deep clean]';

const pricingSection = {
  heading: 'What a deep clean costs',
  body: [
    `A deep clean is priced on the size of the place and the condition it's in, not on a flat hourly rate that would be wrong for most houses. A three bedroom, two bathroom home in reasonable condition sits around ${pricingBand}. If it's been a while, it runs higher, and you'll hear that when we quote rather than on the invoice.`,
    'What moves the number: how many bathrooms, whether the kitchen needs the rangehood and cupboard interiors done, how much is stored on the floor, and whether anything from the quoted-separately list is going in.',
    "You get the figure back fast, based on your actual place rather than a guess. And if a regular clean would serve you better than a deep one, we'll tell you that before you book rather than after.",
  ],
};

// §12 Where we clean — the shared 56-town, three-region list from
// src/data/locations.ts, which every "Where we clean" section now reads
// from. This page specified it first; house-cleaning.astro and the homepage
// then carried verbatim copies, so it was extracted. The open questions —
// the /locations/<slug>/ URL pattern, the ~41 town pages that don't exist
// yet, and the header mega-menu's shorter list — are flagged there, where
// one fix serves every page.

// §13 FAQs — ten questions, ONE block.
//
// Every other page on this site splits its FAQs into a "branded" and a
// "non-branded" accordion, because those briefs supplied two labelled lists.
// This brief supplies one H2 and one run of ten questions, so it ships as one
// accordion. Don't split it back into two for site symmetry — that would mean
// inventing the second heading and deciding which questions sit under it,
// neither of which is in the brief.
//
// Three answers carry [CONFIRM] markers in the brief. The markers are
// stripped from the published copy (they're notes to Teagan, not text for a
// visitor) and each is flagged inline below. All three ship as written,
// unlike the first build's "[X] days", which was a literal unfilled blank —
// these are finished sentences awaiting a read-through.
//
// In `faqItems`, on "How do I know it'll actually be a deep clean and not a quick once-over?":
// No longer over-promising: §5 above IS the published task list. This was
// 🚩2 in the first build's header.
// In `faqItems`, on "How long does a deep clean take?":
// ⚠️ [CONFIRM] — the brief opens this answer with the marker. "Most of a
// day for a team" for a three-bedroom home is an operational estimate
// that sets a client's expectation on the day; whoever schedules these
// jobs needs to agree with it.
// In `faqItems`, on "How much does a deep clean cost?":
// Two answers, one flag. Once §10b's pricing section ships this question
// repeats it, so the confirmed version stops re-explaining and sends the
// reader up to the actual figure instead. While `pricingConfirmed` is
// false that section does not exist and a pointer to it would be a dead
// reference, so the original self-contained answer stands.
// In `faqItems`, on "What isn't included in a deep clean?":
// ⚠️ [CONFIRM the full exclusions list.] This restates §5b's Callout —
// the same fact in two places, so they have to be settled together and
// kept in step. If one changes and the other doesn't, the page
// contradicts itself about what a client is paying for.
// In `faqItems`, on "Do cleaners move furniture during a deep clean?":
// The first build held this back as an unconfirmed safety-and-liability
// claim about what staff will lift. The new brief carries no [CONFIRM] on
// it, which reads as that confirmation having happened — still worth one
// sentence of verification with Teagan, since it's the only answer on the
// page about physical risk to floors and to the team.
// In `faqItems`, on "Do you bring your own products and equipment?":
// ⚠️ [CONFIRM product range.] The brief's marker. The answer makes a
// two-part promise — everything is brought, and preferences can be
// accommodated — and the second half is the one to check: it commits TLB
// to using a client's nominated products.
// In `faqItems`, on "Do you deep clean apartments and units?":
// Added 14 Sep 2026. `apartment deep clean` is 140/mo, up roughly 80%
// year on year, with a local pack firing, and the page spoke only about
// houses. The strata sentence is the half that earns the question: lift
// bookings and contractor access rules are what actually goes wrong on
// the day.

// §14 Closing block — in the brief this time, so it's no longer this build's
// own addition echoing the hero. Real copy, split at the sentence break: the
// promise as the heading, the ask as the lead.
// Reworked 14 Sep 2026. The heading takes the same one-word fix as the H1, so
// the page opens and closes on the term. The second sentence of the lead
// lands the BFD §1.5 payoff the whole page has been building toward and had
// never quite said out loud.

// CONTENT lives in src/content/pages/house-cleaning/deep-cleaning.yaml and is
// edited in Pages CMS ("Deep cleaning", under House cleaning). This file owns
// the layout. The notes above explain why each section says what it says;
// they stay here because Pages CMS rewrites the YAML on save and would drop
// them.
const page = await getPage(
  'house-cleaning/deep-cleaning',
  z.object({
    seo: section.seo,
    hero: section.hero,
    // Its background photo sits behind the trust bar and this block together
    // (the Backdrop below), so it cannot be left empty.
    definition: section.textBlock.extend({ backgroundImage: section.backgroundPhoto }),
    pathways: section.pathwayCards,
    inclusions: section.textGrid,
    exclusions: section.callout,
    moments: section.textGrid,
    comparison: section.comparisonTable,
    beforeAfter: section.beforeAfter,
    video: section.videoFeature,
    // Body is the page's own paragraphs, starting with its page-local
    // "Managed for you" standfirst. The "How it works" steps below them come
    // from Brand lines, so they stay the same on every page.
    booking: section.textBlock,
    reviews: section.sectionHeading,
    whereWeClean: section.tagCloud,
    faq: section.faq,
    closingCta: section.callToAction,
  }),
);

const pageMeta = {
  ...page.seo,
  // Domain still a placeholder, same as the other pages. Confirm before launch.
  canonical: 'https://tlbcleaning.com.au/house-cleaning/deep-cleaning/',
};

const business = localBusiness({
  description: pageMeta.description,
  areaServed: 'Northern Rivers NSW, the Tweed and Southern Gold Coast QLD',
  sameAs: googleBusinessProfileUrl ? [googleBusinessProfileUrl] : [],
});

const heroImage = page.hero.image && sized(page.hero.image, '4/3');

const pathwayCards = page.pathways.cards.map((card, i) => ({
  number: i + 1,
  ...card,
  image: sized(card.image, '4/3'),
}));

// The last inclusions cell spans both columns when the count is odd, so the
// grid closes on a full-width row instead of an orphan (see §5 above).
const inclusionBlocks = page.inclusions.items.map((item, i, all) => ({
  type: 'text' as const,
  ...item,
  ...(all.length % 2 === 1 && i === all.length - 1 ? { span: 2 } : {}),
}));
const momentsBlocks = page.moments.items.map((item) => ({ type: 'text' as const, ...item }));

// The deep clean (the first column) is the highlighted one.
const comparisonColumns = highlightColumn(page.comparison.columns, 0);

// FAQ 5's answer for when `pricingConfirmed` flips. Since the move to Pages
// CMS the live answer is in the content file, so this swap finds the question
// by its wording. If an editor has reworded the question, update the
// content file's answer by hand when the price lands.
const pricedFaqAnswer =
  "It depends on the size of the house and the condition it's in. There's a price guide further up this page, and you'll have a firm number back quickly once you tell us the basics. No obligation attached.";
const faqItems = pricingConfirmed
  ? page.faq.items.map((item) =>
      item.question === 'How much does a deep clean cost?' ? { ...item, answer: pricedFaqAnswer } : item,
    )
  : page.faq.items;
---

<Base title={pageMeta.title} description={pageMeta.description} canonical={pageMeta.canonical} business={business}>
  <SiteHeader
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: logoImg }}
    primaryNav={primaryNav}
    serviceNav={headerNav}
    secondaryCta={headerSecondaryCta}
    cta={quoteCta}
  />

  <main id="main">
    <Hero
      variant="split-single-image"
      imagePosition="right"
      kicker={page.hero.kicker}
      headingLines={page.hero.headingLines}
      subheading={page.hero.subheading}
      lead={page.hero.lead}
      cta={page.hero.cta ?? pageQuoteCta}
      image={heroImage}
    />

    {/* Trust bar + definition block share ONE photo behind a Cream scrim, so it is
        laid out across both — see the homepage for why a per-section
        background cannot do this. 90%, raised from 75% on 29 Sep 2026.
        Measured over this frame's own darkest region under the copy column,
        75% gave 4.22:1 — an AA FAIL. 90% gives 5.96:1. Backdrop.astro's
        `opacity` doc carries the method. Re-check it if the photo is swapped
        in Pages CMS. */}
    <Backdrop image={page.definition.backgroundImage} tint="muted" opacity={90}>
      <TrustBar variant="card" cards={trustBarCards} />
      <TextBlock
        theme="muted"
        align="left"
        heading={page.definition.heading}
        body={page.definition.body}
        cta={page.definition.cta}
      />
    </Backdrop>

    <PathwayCards heading={page.pathways.heading} headingAccent={page.pathways.headingAccent} cards={pathwayCards} />

    <ContentGrid
      columns={2}
      heading={page.inclusions.heading}
      lead={page.inclusions.lead}
      blocks={inclusionBlocks}
    />

    <Callout tone="note" heading={page.exclusions.heading} body={page.exclusions.body} cta={page.exclusions.cta} />

    {/* Heading rewritten 14 Sep 2026. "When people book one" referred back
        to a heading the reader may have scrolled past and carried no
        keyword; the new one works as a standalone answer, which is what
        BFD §14 asks of every service-page section. */}
    <ContentGrid columns={3} heading={page.moments.heading} lead={page.moments.lead} blocks={momentsBlocks} />

    {/* Wrapper carries the #compare anchor PathwayCards card 3 links to —
        ComparisonTable takes no id prop, and adding one just for an anchor
        target isn't worth a shared-component change. */}
    <div id="compare">
      <ComparisonTable
        heading={page.comparison.heading}
        lead={page.comparison.lead}
        cornerLabel={page.comparison.cornerLabel}
        columns={comparisonColumns}
        rows={page.comparison.rows}
        footnote={page.comparison.footnote}
        cta={page.comparison.cta}
      />
    </div>

    {/* Drag the handle to wipe between the two frames of each pair — see
        the block above `beforeAfterPairs` for why this is a slider and not
        a gallery. Both pairs are real TLB job photography and the `note`
        says so; correct it, do not delete it, if a sample ever returns.

        Heading rewritten 14 Sep 2026: "the difference, on real jobs"
        asserted the jobs were real while the images are labelled
        illustrative. The new one carries the keyword and describes what the
        module actually shows. */}
    <BeforeAfter
      heading={page.beforeAfter.heading}
      lead={page.beforeAfter.lead}
      pairs={page.beforeAfter.pairs}
      note={page.beforeAfter.note}
      beforeLabel={page.beforeAfter.beforeLabel}
      afterLabel={page.beforeAfter.afterLabel}
    />

    <VideoFeature headingLines={page.video.headingLines} video={{ ...page.video.video, ratio: '16/9' }} />

    <TextBlock
      theme="dark"
      align="left"
      heading={page.booking.heading}
      body={[...page.booking.body, ...howItWorks.general]}
      cta={page.booking.cta ?? pageQuoteCta}
      backgroundImage={page.booking.backgroundImage}
    />

    {/* §10b, gated on `pricingConfirmed` — see the block above
        `pricingSection`. Renders nothing until Teagan's price band lands,
        and FAQ 5 swaps to point at it on the same flag. */}
    {pricingConfirmed && (
      <TextBlock theme="muted" align="left" heading={pricingSection.heading} body={pricingSection.body} />
    )}

    <GoogleReviewSlider heading={page.reviews.heading} reviews={googleReviews} profileUrl={googleBusinessProfileUrl} />

    {/* The Tweed was being folded into its neighbours. BFD §2.1 lists it
        separately on purpose: Kingscliff, Pottsville and Tweed Heads are
        neither Northern Rivers nor Gold Coast to anyone who lives there, and
        grouping them wrongly is the small error that tells a local you don't
        know the area.

        ✅ The GROUPS below name all three regions again, so this line and the
        list finally agree. They disagreed for two days in September 2026,
        while locations.ts ran a curated list that hid the Tweed entirely and
        then folded four of its towns under the NSW heading. The client's
        18 Sep call — name every mega-menu town, link only the live pages —
        closed it. */}
    <TagCloud
      heading={page.whereWeClean.heading}
      subheading={page.whereWeClean.subheading}
      tags={page.whereWeClean.tags}
      groups={locationGroups}
      note={page.whereWeClean.note}
    />

    {/* The generic heading was identical on every service page, which gives
        Google and an AI assistant nothing to tell them apart. */}
    <Faq heading={page.faq.heading} lead={page.faq.lead} items={faqItems} />

    <CallToAction
      variant="secondary"
      kicker={page.closingCta.kicker}
      heading={page.closingCta.heading}
      lead={page.closingCta.lead}
      cta={page.closingCta.cta ?? pageQuoteCta}
    />
  </main>

  <SiteFooter
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: footerLogoImg }}
    columns={[
      { title: 'Services', links: footerServiceLinks },
      { title: 'Locations', links: [{ label: 'View all locations', href: '/locations/' }] },
    ]}
    contact={footerContact}
    copyright={footerCopyright}
  />
</Base>
